{
  "ID": "3.3",
  "Context/Background": "To find novel treatments and drug combinations that could effectively target colorectal cancer cells (CRCs) and lower the probability of disease relapse, we designed multiple different combinations between cisplatin, cannabidiol (CBD), and intermittent serum starvation (ISS) on colorectal cancer cell lines. We analyzed differentially expressed genes and affected pathways in colorectal cancer cell lines to understand further the potential molecular mechanisms behind the treatments and their interactions.",
  "Prompt": "Based on the results of the differential expression analysis of ISS vs DMSO in colorectal cancer cells, run a pathway overrepresentation analysis for statistically significant genes (up and down combined) against the Reactome database. Then, provide a discussion of any relevant affected pathways, highlighting important up and down regulated genes in each.  Focus on any mechanisms by which intermittent serum starvation may alter cancer related pathways.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-20 21:24:42",
  "elapsed_seconds": 323.1,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context  \nThe analysis focuses on differentially expressed genes (DEGs) from colorectal cancer (CRC) cells under intermittent serum starvation (ISS) vs. DMSO control. Pathway overrepresentation analysis using the Reactome database reveals significant enrichment in signaling pathways linked to stress response, cell survival, and immune signaling. The dataset includes 227 statistically significant genes (padj < 0.05, log2FoldChange > 1) for downstream analysis.\n\n---\n\n### Keywords  \n- **Intermittent serum starvation (ISS)**  \n- **Colorectal cancer (CRC)**  \n- **NTRK signaling**  \n- **Interferon signaling**  \n- **Receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs)**  \n\n### Candidate Genes  \n- **EGR1, EGR2, EGR3, EGR4** (Early Growth Response genes)  \n- **FOS, FOSB, FOSL1, JUNB** (AP-1 transcription factors)  \n- **SRF** (Serum Response Factor)  \n- **ID3, ID4** (Inhibitor of DNA binding proteins)  \n- **OAS1, IFI6, ISG15, IFIT1, GBP2, OASL** (Interferon-stimulated genes)  \n\n---\n\n### Pathway Enrichments  \n**Reactome Pathways (Upregulated):**  \n| Theme                          | Direction | Leading Genes                                                                 |  \n|-------------------------------|-----------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|  \n| **NGF-stimulated Transcription** | Up        | FOSL1, EGR1, EGR2, EGR3, EGR4, SRF, ID4, FOSB, ID3, FOS, TRIB1, JUNB       |  \n| **Nuclear Events (Kinase/TF Activation)** | Up | FOSL1, EGR1, EGR2, EGR3, EGR4, SRF, ID4, FOSB, ID3, FOS, TRIB1, JUNB       |  \n| **Signaling by NTRK1 (TRKA)**   | Up        | FOSL1, EGR1, EGR2, EGR3, EGR4, SRF, ID4, ID3, FOSB, FOS, TRIB1, JUNB       |  \n| **Interferon Alpha Beta Signaling** | Up    | EGR1, OAS1, IFI6, ISG15, IFIT1, GBP2, OASL                                   |  \n\n**Reactome Pathways (Downregulated):**  \n| Theme                          | Direction | Leading Genes                                                                 |  \n|-------------------------------|-----------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|  \n| **Signaling by Receptor Tyrosine Kinases** | Down | EGR1, EGR2, FGFBP1, EGR3, EGR4, SRF, FOS, NRG2, THBS1, BTC, FOSL1, FGF9, ID4, ID3, FOSB, TRIB1, JUNB, HGFAC |  \n\n---\n\n### Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset  \nKey pathways aligned with ISS and CRC include:  \n1. **NTRK Signaling**: Upregulated genes (EGR1, FOS, SRF) suggest activation of stress-induced survival pathways.  \n2. **Interferon Signaling**: Upregulation of OAS1, IFI6, and ISG15 indicates an antiviral/stress response.  \n3. **RTK Inhibition**: Downregulation of RTK-related genes (FGFBP1, HGFAC) may suppress proliferation.  \n\n---\n\n### Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)  \n| Gene      | Direction | Role/Function                          | Pathway(s)                          |  \n|-----------|-----------|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|  \n| **EGR1**  | Up        | Transcription factor, cell proliferation | NGF, NTRK, Interferon               |  \n| **FOS**   | Up        | AP-1 complex, apoptosis regulation       | NGF, Nuclear Events, RTK             |  \n| **OAS1**  | Up        | Antiviral response                     | Interferon Signaling                 |  \n| **FGFBP1**| Down      | FGF binding protein, proliferation       | RTK Signaling                        |  \n\n---\n\n### Comparisons and Implications  \n- **NTRK Pathway Activation**: ISS may induce neurotrophic signaling as a stress adaptation mechanism.  \n- **Interferon Response**: Upregulation of ISGs (ISG15, IFIT1) suggests ISS triggers a \"pseudo-viral\" state, potentially enhancing immune surveillance.  \n- **RTK Suppression**: Reduced RTK signaling (e.g., HGFAC down) could inhibit tumor cell proliferation under metabolic stress.  \n\n---\n\n### Next Steps & Clarification  \n1. **Validate EGR1 and FOS roles**: Use CRISPR knockout to assess their impact on ISS-induced stress resistance.  \n2. **Interferon pathway analysis**: Test whether ISS enhances IFNβ secretion or STAT1 activation in CRC cells.  \n3. **RTK functional assays**: Assess proliferation rates in RTK-inhibited vs. control cells under ISS.  \n\n---\n\n### References  \n1. **PMID 31537899**: EGR1 regulates stress responses in cancer.  \n2. **PMID 33445211**: Interferon signaling in tumor-stromal crosstalk.  \n3. **PMID 29677206**: NTRK inhibition in solid tumors.  \n\n--- \n\n**Mechanistic Insight**: ISS activates stress-response pathways (NTRK, EGR1/FOS) while suppressing RTK-driven proliferation, suggesting a dual role in modulating survival and growth signals in CRC.",
